# What failed is looked for in the line of the place, and a minifier leaves a whole function in one line:
#   // note
#   run(function live(){a.b;c.d;e.f},function dead(){a.b;c.d});{g.h;i.j}
# In a long line the front end says now and then where a statement begins and whose it is: here `c.d;` of `live`, `c.d` of `dead`, and `i.j` of the body of the file.
# The image lists them where the text is kept: the start of the text of the function, and the start of the statement. Nobody enters `dead`, so its text is not kept, and nothing is listed for it.
# A file with the same text shares it. It may list other statements: `e.f` of `live`, and `i.j` again, which is listed once.
# RUN: tables function-texts
# CHECK: 4..32 "function live(){a.b;c.d;e.f}"
# CHECK-NOT: dead
# CHECK: statement of the function at 4 at 24
# CHECK-NEXT: statement of the function at 4 at 28
# CHECK-NEXT: statement of the body at 40
# CHECK-NOT: statement

module m0 path="/a.js" displayPath="a.js" body=f0 positions={lineStarts=[0, 8] textOfBody="\x08\x04\x1c\x01\x18\x0b" startsOfStatementsInLongLines="\x20\x14\x1d\x14\x0b\x00"} functionText="// note\x0arun(function live(){a.b;c.d;e.f},function dead(){a.b;c.d});{g.h;i.j}"
